/* ═══ TERMINAL PHOSPHOR — the sidebar HUD stylesheet ══════════════════════════
   A character-cell amber terminal. See plans/phosphor-hud.md (the binding
   contract) and mockups/hud/04-terminal-phosphor.{html,md} (the visual target).

   Three invariants govern this whole file. Break any one of them and the design
   stops being this design:

   1. THERE IS NOT ONE `border` DECLARATION HERE, and there must never be. Every
      frame stroke on screen — `─ │ ┌ ┐ └ ┘ ├ ┤ ┬ ┴ ┼` — is a real Unicode
      box-drawing glyph emitted inside a text run by a producer in
      src/hud_phosphor_{panels,strips}.cpp, on the cell grid, so junctions
      actually close and a section title can interrupt its own rule
      (`──┤ SOMA ├──`) for zero extra rows. A CSS border cannot do either, and a
      1dp hairline beside a 1-cell glyph stroke reads as two different grammars.
      The audit is `^\s*border` on the declarations, not a bare `grep border`:
      the region block below legitimately sets `box-sizing: border-box`, which
      is the box model and not a stroke. Verified zero on this file.

   2. HIERARCHY IS LUMINANCE, NEVER A SECOND HUE. One hue (DEC P3 amber
      #ffb000) as the seven-rung `ph-*` ladder in data/gui/theme.json. Every
      colour below is a `ph-*` token; the only non-token values are the
      zero-alpha gradient stops, which use the `ph-veil-0` token because token
      substitution is textual and a stop cannot derive an alpha from a token.
      (Do NOT write a token in its doubled-brace form inside a comment here:
      `ui_theme::substitute_tokens` rewrites the file as plain text and does not
      skip comments, so an example spelling is looked up like a real token, warns
      `unknown rcss token`, and lights RmlUi's beacon in the corner of the screen.
      That is a real 20x20px yellow badge this file put on screen for a while.)
      Desaturating this document must be a no-op on the information — that is
      what makes the register colourblind-proof by construction instead of by
      audit, and it only holds if nothing here introduces a hue.

   3. THE FONT SIZE IS NOT SET HERE. See the note above `.ph-row`. This is the
      single most likely thing for a later maintainer to helpfully "fix".

   Two RCSS-6.2 facts, verified against the fetched engine source under
   out/build/win-rel-deb/_deps/rmlui-src, so nobody re-litigates them:
     · `text-shadow` DOES NOT EXIST. `font-effect: glow(...)` is the
       replacement, and it is the better tool anyway — see §HALATION.
     · No CSS grid, no `::before`/`::after`, no `clip-path`, no
       `mix-blend-mode`. Every gradient decorator does exist, and this file is
       built on them.

   This document deliberately carries NEITHER the `panel` NOR the
   `runic-region` class. `lighting::apply_crt()` sweeps every open document for
   both and drives their `decorator` inline every frame with the procedural
   runic frame and a CRT vignette (rmlui_layer.cpp:874-1070). A HUD region with
   either class would have this stylesheet's ground overwritten 60 times a
   second by a warm-brown ring that belongs to the menus. The HUD's own CRT
   glass is `#hud-crt-scan` / `#hud-crt-curve` below; note it is NOT named
   `crt-overlay`, because apply_crt claims that id too.                       */

body {
    background-color: transparent;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* Source Code Pro's advance is exactly 0.6em, which is the grid's whole
       premise: cell_w == 0.6 * font_size, so a 192-cell row has zero horizontal
       drift. Terminus (theme.rcss's body face) does not have that property. */
    font-family: "Source Code Pro";
    /* One face, one weight, no tracking, for the entire HUD. `letter-spacing`
       and `font-weight` are inherited properties, so these two declarations are
       the fence: any tracking at all breaks `────` into a dotted line, and
       there is no bold face loaded anyway (only SourceCodePro-Regular and
       -Semibold are registered, and the register uses neither weight for
       emphasis — emphasis is a ladder rung or reverse video). */
    letter-spacing: normal;
    font-weight: normal;
    color: {{ph-4}};
}

/* ═══ REGIONS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Nine elements, all absolutely positioned. The six gameplay regions are handed
   an explicit left/top/width/height in dp by `sidebar_hud_apply_rect()`, snapped
   to whole cells out of `hud_phosphor::layout_for`. Not one percentage: laying
   regions out in percentages while the terminal was sized in cells is what put
   the shipping hotbar 6.34 dp off the bottom of the screen with a 6.36 dp
   overlap onto the strip above it.

   `box-sizing: border-box` because the rect C++ hands each region is the whole
   region. `overflow: hidden` is a backstop, not a layout mechanism: producers
   pad to exactly their region width via `hud_phosphor::pad`, and if one ever
   overruns anyway, it clips at the rect instead of painting across the map.

   There is no padding on any region. Insets are CELLS, spent inside the text
   rows by the producers (the internal column grids in
   04-terminal-phosphor.md §Layout), because a dp inset would push the grid off
   the cell lattice and the frame would stop closing. */
#hud-status, #hud-soma, #hud-radar, #hud-dock, #hud-log, #hud-keys, #hud-vehicle {
    position: absolute;
    /* RmlUi elements default to `display: inline` — the familiar
       `div { display: block }` lives in RmlUi's SAMPLE rml.rcss, which this game
       does not link (theme.rcss:99-105). Without this, a region's rows flow
       inline into one wrapped paragraph. */
    display: block;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The data-bound wrapper divs. Every element carrying a `data-rml` in
   sidebar_hud.rml is a plain class-less container, because the PRODUCER emits
   the row elements — it knows its own row count and the document does not, which
   is what lets `hud_log_rows` emit a variable number of rows each carrying its
   own `log-N` id for `hud_anim` to target. So `.ph-row` elements arrive
   as grandchildren of a region, with one unstyled layout box in between, and
   that box has to be neutralised on both axes.

   `display: block` is the load-bearing half: an inline wrapper holding block
   rows is a broken box tree, and RmlUi defaults a div to inline. Zero margin and
   padding is the other half — they are already the initial values, but this is
   the one box in the chain that nobody owns by name, so stating it is what stops
   a future theme-wide `div { padding: … }` from pushing every region's rows off
   the cell lattice `apply_rect` snapped the region to.

   Note that the `.ph-row` rule below is UNQUALIFIED on purpose:
   they must lay out identically whether the producer's row is a grandchild of a
   region (today) or its direct child (if the wrapper is ever removed). Nothing
   in this file assumes the wrapper exists. */
#hud-status > div, #hud-soma > div, #hud-radar > div, #hud-dock > div,
#hud-log > div, #hud-keys > div, #hud-vehicle > div {
    display: block;
    margin: 0dp;
    padding: 0dp;
    /* `white-space: pre` HAS to be here as well as on `.ph-row`,
       and this is not redundancy. RmlUi decides whether to KEEP the whitespace in
       parsed content from the `white-space` of the element it is parsing INTO, and
       `data-rml` injects the producer's markup into this wrapper. At
       `white-space: normal` — the default, and what this class-less div had — every
       run of padding spaces in the injected rows was collapsed to one space at
       parse time, before `.ph-row`'s own `pre` ever got a chance to apply at
       layout. The visible result in-game was every label welded to its value
       (`DAY16Spring12:59:46`, `STR13DEX22INT9PER20`), every column out of
       alignment, and rows wrapping because they no longer fitted their box. The
       cell grid does not survive losing its spaces. */
    white-space: pre;
}

/* ═══ THE CELL GRID ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   `.ph-row` is one text row, and every producer emits one per row. It is
   `white-space: pre` and its padding is U+00A0 rather than U+0020 — see the note
   on the wrapper rule above and on `hud_phosphor::pad`; ordinary spaces are
   trimmed at span boundaries when `data-rml` parses the row.

   ── DO NOT ADD A `font-size` OR A `line-height` HERE. ──
   Both are written from C++ onto the document body every sync by
   `sidebar_hud_apply_rect()`, out of `hud_phosphor::metrics`, and both are
   inherited properties (verified: StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:330,356 register
   line-height and font-size with inherited == true), so the value reaches every
   wrapper, every row and every span inside it. An inline property also outranks
   any stylesheet rule, which is why theme.rcss's `body { font-size: 18dp }` is
   already superseded and why a value written here would be dead weight at best.

   RCSS cannot know the cell size. `metrics_for()` chooses it at runtime from the
   context dimensions, off a legibility-ordered ladder — font_size 20 / 18.3333 /
   16.6667 / 15 dp, cell 12x24 / 11x22 / 10x20 / 9x18 — picking the largest cell
   that still yields the minimum grid, so the viewport tiles with no remainder.
   A 1920x1080 dp context lands on 20 dp and a 160x45 grid; a smaller window does
   not. Any absolute number typed here is therefore wrong on some viewport, and
   the em-relative shortcut is worse than it looks. `cell_h == 1.2 * font_size`
   is exact, not a coincidence of the current ladder: cell_w is 0.6 * font_size
   (Source Code Pro's advance) and cell_h is 2 * cell_w (metrics_for's 1:2 cell
   aspect, chosen so box-drawing stems abut between rows), so the product holds
   on every rung and on any rung anyone adds. `line-height: 1.2` would therefore
   work — and it would hardcode the composition of one fact this file does not
   own with one that metrics_for is free to change, which is precisely the
   coupling to avoid. The 0.6 is the font's; the 1:2 is P1's; the product is
   nobody's to duplicate.

   Leaving both unset is also how the height gets pinned to exactly one cell
   without naming it: a block element containing a single line box is exactly one
   line-height tall, and C++ set line-height to `cell_h`. So `.ph-row` is exactly
   one cell tall, by construction,
   at every viewport size — which is what makes `│` stems abut between rows and
   corners close. An explicit `height` would break that the moment it disagreed
   with the inherited line-height.

   So this is a deliberate, agreed departure from the letter of the contract's
   class table, which says `.ph-row` has "`height` and `line-height` both pinned
   to one cell". Both ARE one cell here; neither is pinned by a declaration,
   because a declaration is the one way to make them disagree. `height: auto`
   resolving to the inherited line-height cannot drift from what `apply_rect`
   computed out of `metrics`, and a typed value can. Reviewed with P1 and P3 and
   backed by contract rule 3 ("nothing else may set a font size"). Do not "fix"
   this back to a hardcoded cell height. */
.ph-row {
    display: block;
    white-space: pre;
}
/* `.ph-block` USED TO LIVE HERE AND IS DELIBERATELY GONE. It was a single element
   holding N rows joined by `\n`, relying on `white-space: pre` to break them. That
   does not work in this engine: `data-rml` parses injected markup with the
   element's computed style, and the element carrying the class is the one being
   created, so the newlines were gone before the rule could apply. In-game every
   SOMA and DOCK row ran together on one line and wrapped at the panel edge. Every
   producer now emits one `.ph-row` per row instead. Do not reintroduce a
   newline-joined block element. */

/* ═══ THE LADDER ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   `hud_phosphor::tint( ink, s )` emits `<span class="ph-iN">s</span>` and
   `invert( s )` emits `<span class="ph-inv">s</span>`. Producers emit CLASSES
   and never an inline colour, so the stylesheet keeps ownership of the palette
   and the F4 Theme tab still edits the HUD live.

   `display: inline` is stated explicitly on every one of them, and it is not
   redundant even though inline is RmlUi's default. The shipping HUD's target-HP
   bar was a permanently empty trough because `.tbar-fill` was a `<span>` with
   `height: 100%` and no `display`, so RmlUi's inline default collapsed the box
   the height was a percentage of — while its sibling `.veh-fuel-fill`, which
   carried `display: block`, worked correctly. Two rules, one file, one of them
   with the declaration and one without: that is what proves it was an oversight
   rather than a choice, and stating the intent here is how it stops recurring. */
.ph-i0 { display: inline; color: {{ph-0}}; }
.ph-i1 { display: inline; color: {{ph-1}}; }
.ph-i2 { display: inline; color: {{ph-2}}; }
.ph-i3 { display: inline; color: {{ph-3}}; }
.ph-i4 { display: inline; color: {{ph-4}}; }
.ph-i5 { display: inline; color: {{ph-5}}; }

/* Glyphs drawn INSIDE an inverted cell. Only ever nested in `.ph-inv`. */
.ph-ik { display: inline; color: {{ph-k}}; }

/* Reverse video — the register's only "shout", and deliberately not a colour.
   A critical body part does not get a hotter hue; its whole row inverts. That
   survives greyscale, every form of colour blindness, and a player who has
   turned saturation off, because the distinguishing feature is a FILLED
   BACKGROUND, which no colour transform can take away. */
.ph-inv {
    display: inline;
    background-color: {{ph-5}};
    color: {{ph-k}};
    /* No halation on an inverted run: the amber glow of {{ph-glow}} around dark
       {{ph-k}} glyphs sitting in a bright cell reads as a smudge, not as bloom.
       Real halation is emitted by the bright GROUND here, which font-effect
       cannot do — so the honest move is to emit none. */
    font-effect: none;
}

/* A tinted run NESTED inside an inverted one collapses to `ph-k`.
   `.ph-inv` sets `color`, but `color` loses to any rule that targets the inner
   span directly, so without this an inverted row would show its nested runs in
   their own ladder rung — amber-on-near-white, the one contrast pair in the
   palette that fails. It is not a hypothetical: `hud_phosphor::bar()` returns
   PRE-TINTED markup (fill in `ink::label`/`ink::datum`, trough in `ink::rule`),
   and the critical body-part row inverts the WHOLE row including its bar, so the
   nesting is the normal case rather than a mistake.

   (0,2,0) beats `.ph-iN`'s (0,1,0). This is also just the definition of `ph-k`
   restated as a rule — "glyphs drawn inside an inverted cell" — so it is correct
   independently of `bar()`, and it means no producer has to remember not to
   nest. `.ph-ik` is deliberately absent from the list: it is already `ph-k` and
   only ever appears in here anyway.

   This does flatten the bar inside an inverted row: fill and trough collapse to
   one ink, so the fill-versus-trough LUMINANCE distinction is gone for that row.
   Accepted deliberately, and not replaced. The glyphs still differ — `█`/`▄`
   against `░` — so the bar stays readable on density alone, which is the
   register's own claim that any single channel can be destroyed and the state
   still reads, holding in the one row where it is load-bearing. The alternative
   is a partial-alpha `ph-k` for the trough, which needs a fourteenth token or a
   hex literal, and neither is worth buying back a distinction the glyph already
   carries. A second hue is of course not on the table. */
.ph-inv .ph-i0, .ph-inv .ph-i1, .ph-inv .ph-i2,
.ph-inv .ph-i3, .ph-inv .ph-i4, .ph-inv .ph-i5 {
    color: {{ph-k}};
}

/* ═══ THE TWO OPAQUE STRIPS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
   `#hud-status` and `#hud-keys` are the only opaque grounds in the HUD, and the
   reason is structural rather than aesthetic.

   This register spends its entire hue budget up front: every distinction on
   screen is a luminance step. A luminance-only encoding cannot survive an
   UNCONTROLLED luminance underneath it — over a snowfield the ph-2/ph-3 rungs
   collapse toward the background, over asphalt the ph-4/ph-5 rungs do, and the
   collapse is invisible to the code producing the text. An opaque ground is
   what fixes the denominator of every contrast ratio in the design, so the
   35-53 8-bit greyscale steps between adjacent rungs are guaranteed rather than
   hoped for.

   These two strips are where that guarantee has to be absolute, because they
   carry the state you must never misread: the status strip holds the world and
   character state plus the safe-mode indicator, and the keys strip is the one
   row that tells you which actions are reachable — including which are NOT,
   encoded as `ink::dead` at contrast 1.41 : 1, a distinction a bright tile
   behind it would erase outright. The floating panels below can afford
   translucency because their content is redundantly encoded (bar length, glyph
   tier, position and inversion all say the same thing); a one-bit indicator has
   no redundancy to fall back on.

   They are also the two full-width edge-anchored regions, so they read as the
   terminal's bezel: opacity is what makes the screen edge the fourth wall
   instead of a floating rectangle. */
#hud-status, #hud-keys {
    background-color: {{ph-0}};
    /* Per-panel scanline pass: 3dp pitch, 2dp lit, 1dp {{ph-scan}} (alpha .30).
       An order of magnitude stronger than the full-screen glass pass, because
       here it is over a controlled opaque ground and costs no tilemap clarity.
       Decorators paint over the background-colour and UNDER the element's
       content, so the scanlines fall on the ground and never across a glyph. */
    decorator: repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
        {{ph-veil-0}} 0dp, {{ph-veil-0}} 2dp, {{ph-scan}} 2dp, {{ph-scan}} 3dp );
}

/* ═══ THE FOUR TRANSLUCENT PANELS ═════════════════════════════════════════════
   `#hud-soma`, `#hud-dock`, `#hud-log` and `#hud-vehicle` float over the world
   on the Sparkline Console treatment (mockups/hud/10-sparkline-console.html:
   `rgba(5,8,10,.60)` + `backdrop-filter: blur(2.5px) saturate(.72)
   brightness(.94)` + a soft mask edge), restated on the amber ground as
   `{{ph-veil}}` (#0c0800a3). They can afford it because their content is
   redundantly encoded — see the note on the opaque strips.

   ── `backdrop-filter` IS DECLARED AND IS NOT YET IMPLEMENTED. ──
   Read that as "not yet implemented", not as "implemented, therefore this must
   be working". The property is real in RmlUi 6.2
   (StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:408; blur/saturate/brightness are all registered
   filters, Factory.cpp:217-225), but this game's render interface,
   `lighting::rmlui_render_interface`, overrides only geometry, textures,
   scissor, transform and shaders. Four methods it does NOT override —
   `PushLayer`, `PopLayer`, `CompositeLayers` and `CompileFilter` — are exactly
   the ones the effect needs, and their base implementations are empty
   (RenderInterface.cpp:26-50). So on today's build:

     · the ALPHA translucency below is real and visible — it is ordinary blend,
       straight through the geometry path;
     · the BLUR, the desaturation and the brightness knock-down are NOT. They
       draw nothing and cost nothing.

   `filter:` is in the same boat and appears nowhere in this file for that
   reason. `font-effect` is unaffected — it is applied in the font engine when
   the glyph bitmap is generated, not through the render interface — so the
   halation in §HALATION is genuinely there. This declaration is kept because it
   is correct RCSS that becomes live for free the day the layer API lands; until
   then it is a known limitation, deliberately traceable from this comment.

   ── THE SOFT EDGE, AND WHY IT IS NOT `mask-image` ──
   `mask-image` exists too (StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:404) but on this backend
   it is not inert, it is destructive, and that asymmetry is worth writing down
   because the property list makes the two look equally safe. At
   RenderStage::Exit, ElementEffects.cpp:295-307 calls `PushLayer()`, renders the
   mask decorators THROUGH THE NORMAL RENDER PATH, then calls
   `SaveLayerAsMaskImage()`. `PushLayer()` returns a null handle and draws
   nothing, so the mask's gradient quads land straight on the swapchain ON TOP OF
   the panel's text, while `SaveLayerAsMaskImage()` also returns null and no
   masking happens at all. There is no safe choice of stop colours either: a mask
   needs an alpha-1 "keep" stop, and any alpha-1 colour painted directly hides
   the panel underneath it.

   So the dissolve is the panel's GROUND instead — a linear-gradient decorator,
   which this backend implements on purpose (rmlui_render_interface.h:79-86:
   "Without these, RmlUi gradient decorators silently render nothing") and which
   `#hud-vignette` has shipped on for months. It is the better artefact
   regardless, and on the merits rather than as a fallback: a mask multiplies the
   element's whole alpha, so it would dim the GLYPHS along with the ground, and
   in a register whose entire encoding is luminance, dimming glyphs at a panel
   edge corrupts the encoding. Fading only the ground leaves every glyph at full
   luminance. The plateau is exactly `{{ph-veil}}` out to 78%, so the result is
   pixel-identical to a flat `background-color: {{ph-veil}}` everywhere except
   the dissolving edge.

   If someone implements the layer API and still wants the literal mask, the
   change is one declaration: restore a flat `background-color: {{ph-veil}}`,
   drop the veil stop-list from `decorator`, and add
   `mask-image: linear-gradient( 180deg, {{ph-5}} 0%, {{ph-5}} 78%,
   {{ph-veil-0}} 100% )`. Two traps if you do. First, a `closest-side` RADIAL
   mask leaves the box CORNERS opaque — the closest-side circle never reaches
   them — so every radial mask paints a square with a soft disc inside it; any
   radial mask must terminate at `100%`, i.e. `farthest-corner`. Second, do not
   put two mask decorators on one element expecting an intersection:
   ElementEffects.cpp:299-304 renders the whole list into ONE layer, so a second
   full-box gradient composites OVER the first and you get a union, which is to
   say no fade on either axis.

   The fades are VERTICAL ONLY, and that is a requirement rather than a
   simplification: `soma`, `dock` and `vehicle` swap sides with
   `SIDEBAR_POSITION` (layout_options::sidebar_right), so a fade hardcoded on a
   panel's left or right edge points into the bezel the moment the player
   mirrors the sidebar. Top and bottom edges do not mirror. */
.ph-veil {
    backdrop-filter: blur(2.5dp) saturate(0.72) brightness(0.94);
    /* Scanline pass over a veil ground that dissolves downward. Listed first =
       painted last = on top (ElementEffects.cpp:195-200 walks the decorator
       list in reverse), so the scanlines sit on the veil rather than under it. */
    decorator:
        repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
            {{ph-veil-0}} 0dp, {{ph-veil-0}} 2dp, {{ph-scan}} 2dp, {{ph-scan}} 3dp ),
        linear-gradient( 180deg,
            {{ph-veil}} 0%, {{ph-veil}} 78%, {{ph-veil-0}} 100% );
}

/* `#hud-radar` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT from `.ph-veil` and has no decorator of its
   own, and that is a requirement rather than an omission. Its content is not text
   in this document at all: it is a field of solid quads that `hud_radar::draw`
   queues onto the UI compositing layer, which composites UNDER RmlUi
   (sdl_render_frame.cpp: world -> ui_target -> HUD particles -> RmlUi). A
   translucent ground laid over it would multiply every dot, and the two dimmest
   rungs of the ladder — open ground and water — would sink into the veil and
   vanish, taking a third of the map's information with them. The radar therefore
   paints its own opaque `ink::ground` interior before its dots, from the same
   token, so the luminance encoding still has a controlled background. Do not add
   `class="ph-veil"` to it in the .rml, and do not give it a `decorator` here. */

/* The log dissolves UPWARD, the other three downward. The log is the only
   bottom-anchored panel — its newest message is on its last row, hard against
   the keys strip whose rule closes the log's frame from below — so a downward
   fade would dissolve exactly the line the player most needs to read. Running
   it the other way puts the dissolve on the OLDEST rows, where it lands on top
   of the producers' `ph-2 → ph-5` recency ramp and reinforces it: the phosphor
   persistence curve and the panel edge fade out together, in the same place, for
   the same reason. ID (1,0,0) beats `.ph-veil` (0,1,0). */
#hud-log {
    decorator:
        repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
            {{ph-veil-0}} 0dp, {{ph-veil-0}} 2dp, {{ph-scan}} 2dp, {{ph-scan}} 3dp ),
        linear-gradient( 0deg,
            {{ph-veil}} 0%, {{ph-veil}} 78%, {{ph-veil-0}} 100% );
}

/* ═══ MESSAGE LOG ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The log region is sized to its line count by `hud_phosphor::layout_for`
   (layout_options::log_lines), so ALL of the old scroll machinery is gone:
   `.hud-scroll`, `.log-well`, `.log-fade`, `.log-fade-top`, the
   `#hud-log-body scrollbarvertical` rules, and the zero-basis flex chain that
   existed only to give `overflow-y` a bounded viewport to clip against.

   That machinery was managing an emptiness. The shipping log well was 752 dp of
   container holding 267 dp of message — 485 dp, 45% of the dock's own height and
   7.6% of the entire screen, spent on empty trough, which is more area than
   everything this redesign adds. Reclaiming it is where the occlusion saving
   comes from, and it is why this design carries every field in the fixture at
   28.80% coverage against the shipping HUD's ratified 31.02%.

   Nothing replaces the age ramp either. The luminance ramp across
   `.ph-i2` … `.ph-i5` IS the phosphor-persistence curve; `hud_log_rows` picks
   the rung per message, so the ladder classes above are its entire
   implementation. Recency and severity now ride on different channels (rung vs.
   glyph tier) instead of both reaching for opacity, so they cannot contend.

   `hud_log_rows` emits a titled top rule and then one `.ph-row` per message,
   each carrying `hud-log-entry` and a stable `log-<seq>` id, plus
   `hud-log-fresh` on the newest. Those two classes need exactly one declaration
   between them, below.

   `.hud-log-fresh` is deliberately given NOTHING. Its predecessor drew a 2dp
   accent rule down the row's left edge and washed the ground behind it — which
   is a CSS border and a second grammar, in a design where the newest message is
   already the brightest thing in the panel (`ph-5`, against `ph-2` for the
   oldest) and already carries a `>` marker and a solid cursor block from the
   producer. Three encodings is enough; a fourth in a different vocabulary is
   noise. The class stays in the markup as `hud_anim`'s handle. */
.hud-log-entry {
    /* The `hud_log_entry` spec animates offset_y, which `hud_anim` applies as
       `top` — inert on a statically positioned element. hud_anim does set
       position:relative itself on the first non-zero frame, but declaring it
       here keeps the slide from depending on that detail. */
    position: relative;
}

/* ═══ CRT GLASS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Two full-screen artefact layers, above the regions and below the damage
   vignette, neither of them hit-testable. They are the only elements in this
   file that paint over the play area, and both are deliberately weak enough that
   the tilemap, the mobs and the player sprite stay fully legible under them —
   which is why 04-terminal-phosphor.md §Occlusion reports them separately
   rather than folding them into the occlusion budget.

   z-index 10 and 11 are the theme's established overlay rungs (theme.rcss `.crt`
   uses 10, apply_crt's injected `#runic-frame` uses 11), and `#hud-vignette`
   keeps 20, so the stack from the world upward is: regions, scanline glass,
   faceplate curve, damage flash. */
#hud-crt-scan, #hud-crt-curve {
    position: absolute;
    display: block;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
}

/* Scanlines over the whole faceplate, glass and all: the same 3dp pitch as the
   per-panel pass but at {{ph-scan-glass}} (alpha .075 against .30) — an order of
   magnitude weaker, on purpose. The per-panel pass sits on a ground this
   stylesheet controls; this one sits on the tilemap, where every lost contrast
   step is a sprite the player has to squint at. Mean darkening is ~2.5%: enough
   to read as a CRT, not enough to soften a tile edge. */
#hud-crt-scan {
    z-index: 10;
    decorator: repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
        {{ph-veil-0}} 0dp, {{ph-veil-0}} 2dp,
        {{ph-scan-glass}} 2dp, {{ph-scan-glass}} 3dp );
}

/* The faceplate curve: the corner darkening a real tube gets from its own
   geometry. Clear to 42%, then ramping to {{ph-vignette}} (alpha .458) at the
   corners. `farthest-corner` is load-bearing — it is what makes the ramp
   actually reach the corners; a `closest-side` radial would leave them at the
   final stop's colour as a hard square (see the mask-image note above). The
   centre defaults to 50% 50%, so it is left unstated.

   The mockup ramps in two segments (transparent → .18 at 72% → .46 at 100%).
   This is one segment, because the ladder has exactly one dark-scrim token and
   the alternative is a hex literal — which would be the only non-token colour in
   a file whose whole argument is that there are none. The cost is a slightly
   deeper mid-field than the mockup's curve; the corners are identical. */
#hud-crt-curve {
    z-index: 11;
    decorator: radial-gradient( farthest-corner, {{ph-veil-0}} 42%, {{ph-vignette}} 100% );
}

/* ═══ HALATION ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Light scattering inside the faceplate, on every text-bearing element.

   The mockup gets this by duplicating the ENTIRE glyph layer, blurring the copy
   and painting it under the crisp one — rows 53-102 of
   04-terminal-phosphor.html are a byte-identical copy of rows 0-50 for exactly
   this reason. That is not a technique, it is a workaround: a browser has no
   per-glyph glow primitive, so the only way to get bleed proportional to emitted
   luminance was to blur a copy that already carried the ladder steps.

   RmlUi has `font-effect` natively, so the copy is unnecessary — which halves
   the text this HUD lays out and rasterises, and removes an entire class of
   desynchronisation between two copies of the same content. It is also the one
   effect in this file that is fully implemented on the current backend:
   `font-effect` is applied in the font engine when the glyph bitmap is
   generated, not through the render interface, so unlike `backdrop-filter` it
   needs no layer API. `font-effect` is inherited
   (StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:405), so declaring it on the row and block
   elements reaches every ladder span inside them.

   `text-shadow` is NOT the alternative here — it does not exist in RCSS 6.2, at
   any spelling. If you find yourself reaching for it, `font-effect: glow(
   <width> <colour> )` is the property you want. */
.ph-row {
    font-effect: glow( 2dp {{ph-glow}} );
}

/* ═══ VEHICLE PANEL ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Same register as the other floating panels, on `.ph-veil`, and reduced to
   nothing but that: `hud_veh_panel` emits cell-exact `.ph-row` elements like every
   other producer, so the whole old flex chain (`.veh-panel-inner`, `.veh-row`,
   `.veh-name`, `.veh-heading`, `.veh-fuels`, `.veh-fuel-row`, `-label`, `-bar`,
   `-fill`, `-pct`) is gone along with its min-width/max-width guesses and its
   1dp box. Its fuel gauges are eighth-block bars now, at sub-cell precision the
   old 60dp-wide div could not reach.

   `display: none` is the resting state and must stay: C++ toggles this region's
   display when the player takes or leaves vehicle controls, and `layout_for`
   only reserves cells for it when `show_vehicle` is set — so a visible-by-
   default panel would sit on top of the dock's rows on foot. This declaration
   comes after the region block above, which sets `display: block`, and both are
   at (1,0,0), so source order is what decides it. Keep it here, below. */
#hud-vehicle {
    display: none;
}

/* ═══ DAMAGE VIGNETTE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Kept fully working, including the `opacity: 0` resting state: `hud_anim` owns
   this element (`panels.cpp` feeds it under the `hud_vignette` spec on an HP
   drop) and drives its opacity through a Penner curve. Nothing in this file may
   write `opacity` here, and nothing does — see the transition note below.

   Recoloured, though. It was the last second hue in the document: a red
   `#e0505099` radial, a literal that shadowed no token, in a design whose
   central claim is that every distinction is luminance on one hue. A red flash
   would also be the one piece of feedback in the HUD that a protanope reads
   differently from everyone else. The register's own answer to "you were just
   hit" is a phosphor overload — the tube blooming amber at its edges — so the
   corners now ramp to {{ph-4}}, the datum rung, from the zero-alpha twin at the
   centre. Same geometry, same channel, same animation, one fewer hue. */
#hud-vignette {
    position: absolute;
    display: block;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 20;
    opacity: 0;
    decorator: radial-gradient( farthest-corner, {{ph-veil-0}}, {{ph-4}} );
}

/* ═══ ENVIRONMENTAL RESPONSE, AND WHY THERE IS NO `transition` ═════════════════
   Two findings from plans/hud-coherence.md §1.8 and §Phase 5 decided this
   section, and the decision is deliberate in both directions.

   ── THE THREE HUE TINTS ARE GONE, NOT FIXED. ──
   `.env-fire`, `.env-rad` and `.env-cold` repainted each region's ground with a
   pre-blended hue. Three of the five environmental tints had in fact never
   rendered: each region set `background-color` through an ID selector (1,0,0)
   and the tints tried to override it from a bare class selector (0,1,0), so they
   lost the cascade outright. The mechanical fix is trivial — compound
   `#hud-soma.env-fire` is (1,1,0) and wins — and it is the wrong fix, because
   the bug was hiding a design error.

   An environmental HUE tint is incoherent by construction in this register. The
   whole design is the claim that there is exactly one hue on screen and that
   every distinction is therefore a luminance step; a fire-orange or radiation-
   green ground introduces precisely the second hue the ladder exists to
   eliminate, and it does it by moving the GROUND, which is the denominator of
   every contrast ratio in the file. Reviving three dead tints would have been
   three new ways to break the accessibility property the register is built on.
   They are styled nowhere below; if `apply_env_classes` still sets those
   classes, they are inert, and that is the intended end state.

   ── NO LUMINANCE RESPONSE EITHER, FOR NOW. ──
   An earlier draft kept `.env-night` / `.env-storm` here as an `opacity` step on
   the four floating panels, on the grounds that "the HUD becomes more
   transparent to a dark world" is a luminance response rather than a hue one.
   Both rules are gone, for two reasons that only became clear once the C++ side
   landed:

   1. Nothing sets the classes. `sidebar_hud_sync` used to apply `.env-*` to the
      Qud-era region ids, and that block was deleted along with those ids. Live
      rules that no C++ path can ever trigger are how the three dead hue tints
      above survived for as long as they did.
   2. `opacity` is the wrong channel regardless. It multiplies the whole subtree,
      so it scales every rung of the ladder toward the backdrop at once — which
      compresses exactly the luminance separation this register's accessibility
      claim rests on. And it is the one channel `hud_anim` also writes inline, so
      even a static declaration here puts the two systems on the same property.

   A night response is still worth having; it just has to move the GROUND's alpha
   rather than the element's opacity, so the rungs keep their separation and the
   panel gets more transparent instead of the text getting dimmer. That is a new
   feature with a producer side to it, not a port of the old one, so it is out of
   scope here and deliberately absent rather than half-present.

   ── THERE IS NO `transition` IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. ──
   The shipping HUD carried `transition: background-color 0.8s, opacity 0.8s` on
   five regions. An RCSS transition wins on the property it names, so that one
   declaration silently overrode every C++ Penner easing curve `hud_anim` writes
   to `opacity` — every flash, every pulse, every log entry tween, on five of six
   regions, replaced by a linear 0.8 s ramp. The recommended repair is to scope
   the transition to `background-color` alone. Applied literally here it is
   inert: once the hue tints are dropped, nothing in this document animates
   `background-color` at all — the two opaque grounds are static `{{ph-0}}` and
   the four panel grounds are static gradients — so a scoped transition would be
   a declaration with no property to act on.

   So there is no transition rule, and `hud_anim` owns every animated channel in
   this document uncontested. Nothing is given up by that here, because with the
   env classes gone there is no slow context shift left to ease: the two opaque
   grounds are static `{{ph-0}}` and the four panel grounds are static gradients.
   If an eased env response is ever wanted it must come from `hud_anim` — a spec
   on these four ids — and never from RCSS, because `opacity` is the one channel
   both systems can reach and an RCSS transition on it is the exact mechanism
   that broke every animation timing in the HUD this replaces. */
